# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Software/contentIdentifierType node980
node980 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>contentIdentifierType</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Software/contentIdentifierType' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Software/contentIdentifierType</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;Specifies the type of the content identifier.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;Specifies the type of the content identifier.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:note</td><td align='left'>&quot;A contentIdentifierType specifies the type of the content identifier.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship node981
node981 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>VexVulnAssessmentRelationship</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;Abstract ancestor class for all VEX relationships.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;Abstract ancestor class for all VEX relationships.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:note</td><td align='left'>&quot;VexVulnAssessmentRelationship is an abstract subclass that defined the common
properties shared by all the SPDX-VEX status relationships.

*Constraints*

When linking elements using a VexVulnAssessmentRelationship, the following
requirements shall be observed:

- The to: end shall point to elements representing the VEX *products*.

To specify a different element where the vulnerability was detected, the VEX
relationship can optionally specify *subcomponents* using the assessedElement
property.

VEX inherits information from the document level down to its statements. When a
statement is missing information it can be completed by reading the equivalent
field from the containing document. For example, if a VEX relationship is
missing data in its createdBy property, tools shall consider the entity
listed in the CreationInfo section of the document as the VEX author.
In the same way, when a VEX relationship does not have a created property,
the document&#x27;s date shall be considered as authoritative.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
